# Why did the price of oil quadruple in 1973?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What does supply and demand say about the oil embargo? Why did the price jump far more than the oil that went missing?"
next: {"title":"How do we measure how rich a country is?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/10-how-do-we-measure-how-rich-a-country-is.cast"}

## A small hole, a giant jump
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 8 Oct 1973
      - 16 Oct 1973
      - 1 Jan 1974
    values:
      - 3.01
      - 5.12
      - 11.65
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    y_label: $ per barrel
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 14
    box:
      x: 120
      'y': 400
      w: 760
      h: 245
    label_size: 22
sources:
    - id: hansard1974
      title: Oil Imports (Price) — written answer, HC Deb 18 January 1974 vol 867 c183W
      authors: UK House of Commons
      year: 1974
      finding: 'Posted price of Arabian Light crude: $3.01 a barrel on 8 October 1973 and $11.65 on 1 January 1974.'
      url: https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written-answers/1974/jan/18/oil-imports-price
    - id: geoexpro
      title: 'GEO ExPro: the 1973 oil crisis (history article)'
      authors: GEO ExPro
      year: 2013
      finding: On 16 October 1973 the six Gulf OPEC producers raised the posted price of marker crude from $3.01 to $5.12, a 70% rise.
      url: https://geoexpro.com/?p=46319
    - id: hamilton2011
      title: Historical Oil Shocks (NBER Working Paper 16790)
      authors: James D. Hamilton
      year: 2011
      finding: Production from Arab members of OPEC in November 1973 was down 4.4 mb/d from September, a shortfall corresponding to 7.5% of global output.
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w16790

    card title "Why did oil quadruple in 1973?"

Why did the price of oil quadruple in 1973? Start with the price the big Gulf producers posted for a barrel of their main crude.
    draw axes

In early October 1973, it was about three dollars.
    draw bar_1

    pause 0.3

On the sixth of October, Egypt and Syria attacked Israel.
    highlight bar_1

(@draw bar_2@) Ten days later the Gulf producers raised their price by about seventy percent, and the next day the Arab produc (@highlight bar_2 effect underline@)ers announced an embargo on countries backing Israel, with cuts in output.

From the first of January 1974: eleven dollars sixty-five.
    draw bar_3

Almost four times where it started.
    text times "×3.9" #b5482e x 870 y 590 font_size 34

    pause 0.5

B: So a huge share of the world's oil must have vanished. A third? Half?
    erase times

Make your own guess first: what share of the world's oil went missing?
    scale missing cites ["hamilton2011"] y 222 label "Share of world oil missing" unit % min 0 max 50 value 7.5 ticks 5

    ask "Click on the line where you think it is." on missing store g default "25" tolerance 0.06
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it was {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it was {g.true}.

At the worst point, in November, Arab producers pumped 4.4 million barrels a day less than in September: about seven and a half percent of the world's oil.
    highlight missing

    pause 0.5

B: Seven and a half percent missing, and the price up almost three hundred percent?
    highlight bar_3

That is th (@highlight missing@)e puzzle: a small hole in supply, a  (@highlight bar_3@)giant jump in price.

To see why, we need the shape of the market in a crisis.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

## Why steep curves jump
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Barrels a day (Q)
    y_label: Price a barrel (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 1.5
    supply:
      elasticity: 1
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    supply_shift:
      amount: 0
    change_arrows: true

    icon car size 80 set twemoji x 230 y 580 icon_look picture of car hidden true
    icon heat size 80 set twemoji x 335 y 580 icon_look picture of house hidden true
    icon plant size 80 set twemoji x 440 y 580 icon_look picture of factory hidden true

    card title "Why steep curves jump"

To answer it we need the supply and demand picture: barrels a day along the bottom, the price of a barrel up the side.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S

    draw equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar parallel true

    draw supply_shift_curve label_S_shift

B: And the embargo pushes supply to the left: at every price, less oil is offered.
    animate supply_shift.amount -12 duration 3

(@draw supply_shift_arrow@) If buyers could easily do without oil (@draw shift_equilibrium_point shift_guide_lines label_E_shift label_P_shift label_Q_shift@), the price would rise only a little, and they would simply buy less.

    pause 0.3

B: But in the autumn of 1973, could they?
    highlight demand_curve

    keep demand_curve shift_equilibrium_point

Not quickly. The car you  (@draw car@)own, the furnace in your b (@draw heat@)asement, the factory's bo (@draw plant@)iler: they burn what they burn for months or years.

Economists call that inelastic demand: the quantity bought hardly responds to the price.
    animate demand.elasticity 0.15 duration 3

    erase car heat plant

    pause 0.5

B: And oil fields outside the Arab world can't pump much more in a few weeks either, so supply is steep too.
    animate supply.elasticity 0.12 duration 3

(@draw change_arrows@) Now the same cut is absorbed almost entirely by the price. It climbs unt (@highlight label_P_shift@)il enough buyers give up enough barrels, and very few can.

    pause 0.5

    erase change_arrows

B: Try it: drag the end of the demand curve to make it flatter or steeper, and watch how far the price must jump for the same cut.
    explore

    fade demand_curve_ghost shift_equilibrium_point_ghost to 0

The steeper the curves, the bigger the jump: a small shortfall with nowhere to go becomes a large price rise.
    highlight label_P_shift shift_guide_lines

## But… not fourfold
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Barrels a day (Q)
    y_label: Price a barrel (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 0.15
    supply:
      elasticity: 0.12
      label: S′
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      label: model
      p_label: P model
      q_label: Q
      shift_label: panic
      shift_p_label: P
      shift_q_label: ''
    demand_shift:
      amount: 0
      label: D′
sources:
    - id: ornl2018
      title: Using Meta-Analysis to Estimate World Crude Oil Demand Elasticity (ORNL/TM-2018/1070)
      authors: Rocío Uría-Martínez, Paul N. Leiby, Gbadebo Oladosu, David C. Bowman, Megan M. Johnson
      year: 2018
      finding: Mean short-run world crude oil demand elasticities range from -0.07 to -0.14; long-run from -0.26 to -0.83.
      url: https://info.ornl.gov/sites/publications/Files/Pub120229.pdf
    - id: yergin1991
      title: 'The Prize: The Epic Quest for Oil, Money, and Power'
      authors: Daniel Yergin
      year: 1991
      finding: In mid-December 1973 Iran held an auction to test the market; bids came in at over $17 a barrel.
    - id: barskykilian2001
      title: Do We Really Know That Oil Caused the Great Stagflation? A Monetary Alternative
      authors: Robert B. Barsky, Lutz Kilian
      year: 2001
      finding: The rise in oil and other commodity prices in the early 1970s owed much to monetary expansion and a global boom, not only to the embargo.
    - id: hamilton2011
      title: Historical Oil Shocks (NBER Working Paper 16790)
      authors: James D. Hamilton
      year: 2011
      finding: While the broader economic context matters, the specific timing, magnitude and nature of the 1973-74 supply cutbacks were closely related to geopolitical events.
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w16790

    scratch calc cites ["ornl2018"] work ["1% less oil → P up ~10%","7.5% less → P up ~75%"] x 800 y 450 font_size 28 hidden true
    math eq tex "\\%\\Delta P \\approx \\frac{\\%\\Delta Q}{\\varepsilon_D + \\varepsilon_S}" size 34 steps ["\\approx \\frac{7.5\\%}{0.1 + \\approx 0}","\\approx 75\\%"] x 800 y 470 hidden true
    text bid "Iran auction: $17+" #b5482e cites ["yergin1991"] x 810 y 400 font_size 28 hidden true
    text war "Hamilton: the war" #2f6b8f cites ["hamilton2011"] x 810 y 420 font_size 28 hidden true
    text boom "Barsky & Kilian: the boom" #b5482e cites ["barskykilian2001"] x 810 y 340 font_size 28 hidden true
    text sure1 "Steep short run:\nmostly agreed" x 810 y 420 font_size 28 hidden true
    text sure2 "Embargo, cartel or boom:\ncontested" #b5482e x 810 y 320 font_size 28 hidden true
    cards mathpick options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after_math"}] then after_math hidden true
    text target "real: ×4" #b5482e x 250 y 260 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c1 "1 · cartel" #2f6b8f x 810 y 610 font_size 28 hidden true
    text c2 "2 · panic" #d0865f x 810 y 565 font_size 28 hidden true
    text c3 "3 · boom" #d0865f x 810 y 520 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "But… not fourfold"

B: (@draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar@) Steep curves explain a big jump. But do they exp (@draw target@)lain a fourfold one?

(@draw calc_box calc_line_1@) Not on their own. Studies put the short-run elasticity o (@highlight demand_curve label_D@)f oil demand at around a tenth: for each one percent less oil, the price must rise roughly ten percent.

(@draw calc_line_2@) So a seven and a half percent cut points to a rise of very roughly seventy- (@highlight label_Pstar@)five percent. Big, but nowhere near three hundred.

    pause 0.5

    erase calc

    draw mathpick

    ask "Want to see where the seventy-five percent comes from?" on mathpick stage own

@math

    erase mathpick

The price change is roughly the quantity change divided by how strongly buyers and sellers respond: the two elasticities added up.
    draw eq

In the short run, supply barely responds, so it is seven and a half percent divided by about a tenth,
    step "eq"

which is about seventy-five percent.
    step "eq"

    erase eq

@after_math

    erase mathpick

Three things filled the gap. First, the price was not set in an open market: the Gulf producers poste (@draw c1@)d it, and OPEC, acting as a cartel, used the moment to set it high.
    highlight supply_curve label_S

B: Then why didn't the oil go unsold at that price?
    highlight equilibrium_point

    draw demand_shift_curve label_D_shift

Because buyers panicked too. Fearing worse, refiners and governments bid to fill their tanks,
    animate demand_shift.amount 5 duration 3

    draw c2

    draw shift_equilibrium_point shift_guide_lines label_E_shift label_P_shift demand_shift_arrow parallel true

and when Iran auctioned oil in December, bids came in above seventeen dollars.
    draw bid

    erase bid

Third, the market was alread (@draw c3@)y tight. Robert Barsky and Lutz Kilian argue that the boom and cheap money of 1971 to 1973 had been pushing up the prices of most raw materials, oil included.
    highlight demand_shift_curve label_D_shift

    pause 0.3

B: So was it the war, or the economics?
    highlight supply_curve demand_shift_curve

    erase c1 c2 c3

(@draw war@) James Hamilton answers that the timing an (@highlight supply_curve@)d size of the cuts were tied to the war;

Barsky and Kilian, that the pressure had been building for years.
    draw boom

    erase war boom

    erase target

How sure are we? Tha (@draw sure1@)t short-run oil demand is steep: mostly agreed.

How much of the fourfold rise was embargo, cartel or boom: contested.
    draw sure2

## Test: steep markets elsewhere
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Petrol a day (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 0.3
    supply:
      elasticity: 0.5
      label: S′
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: P after cut
      q_label: ''
      label: E′
    price_ceiling:
      level: 28
      label: Cap at old price
      show_shortage: true
sources:
    - id: frechlee1987
      title: 'The Welfare Cost of Rationing-by-Queuing Across Markets: Theory and Estimates from the U.S. Gasoline Crises'
      authors: H. E. Frech III, William C. Lee
      year: 1987
      finding: Time spent waiting in queues added about 12% to the cost of gasoline for urban residents in December 1973 and 50% in March 1974 (as summarised by Hamilton 2011).

    scratch wait work ["Shortage → queues","waiting: +12% → +50%"] x 820 y 470 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Test: steep markets elsewhere"

(@draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar@) Steep curves, a cartel and a panic turned a cut of seven and a (@highlight label_Pstar@) half percent into a fourfold price. One more piece of 1973 tests the idea.

B: In the United States, price controls kept oil products near their old prices. What do you predict?
    draw ceiling_line label_ceiling

    quiz "A cap holds the price below where supply and demand now meet. What happens?" voice b
        right: Queues: at the capped price, drivers want far more than sellers offer, so the gap is rationed by waiting.
        wrong: At the capped price, compare how much drivers want with how much is offered.
        + Queues and shortages
        * Nothing: the cap protects drivers
        * Oil companies sell more

(@draw shortage_guide_qs label_Qs@) Queues. At the old price, drivers wanted fa (@draw shortage_guide_qd label_Qd@)r more petrol than there was,

(@draw shortage_arrow wait_box wait_line_1@) so they paid in time instead: for city drivers, one study put the cost of waiting at an extr (@draw wait_line_2@)a twelve percent on the price in December 1973, and fifty percent by March.

    pause 0.5

B: Two more cases: another market, and oil with time to adjust.
    fade shortage_guides shortage_arrow label_Qs label_Qd ceiling_line label_ceiling wait to 0.25

    quiz "A frost destroys 5% of the world's coffee crop. Drinkers barely change their habits for months, and new bushes take years to grow. The price will most likely…" voice b
        right: Far more than 5%: with both curves steep, a small cut can only be cleared by a big price rise.
        wrong: How steep are both curves in the short run?
        * rise by about 5%
        + rise by far more than 5%
        * fall

    quiz "Ten years after an oil cut, people have bought efficient cars and new oil fields have opened. An equal second cut would raise the price…"
        right: Less: given time, buyers and sellers adjust, both curves flatten, and the same cut moves the price less.
        wrong: Given time, how steep do the curves get?
        * more than the first
        + less than the first
        * the same

    erase shortage_guides shortage_arrow label_Qs label_Qd ceiling_line label_ceiling wait

So the price of oil did not quadruple because the oil ran out. A small cut met buyers who could not wait, sellers who could hold out, and a market already stretched.
    highlight equilibrium_point demand_curve supply_curve

